`ARRAYPATH` made separate copies along the curve, even when `ARRAYASSOCIATIVITY` is on, so a path array couldn't be edited afterwards the way rectangular and polar arrays can. It now makes one associative array by default, as `ARRAYRECT` and `ARRAYPOLAR` do: a block reference whose items sit along the path, turned with its tangent unless `align` is off. The array keeps the path as a polyline, starting at the array's base point, with arcs in chords of at most 2 degrees. `ARRAYEDIT` changes the item count, the spacing, divide-evenly versus measure, and alignment (JSON `items`, `spacing`, `divide`, `align`; Items at the command line). `EXPLODE` gives plain objects. The parameters go through DXF in the existing array xdata, because they are serde JSON there. Other programs keep at most 16 KB of xdata per object, so array xdata past 12,000 bytes as written (non-ASCII characters count as their `\U+XXXX` escapes) is left out: the array is then saved as a plain block of its items. A circle path (180 vertices) fits; a long, curvy path can pass the limit. `ARRAYASSOCIATIVITY 0` or `"associative": false` keeps the old copies, unchanged.

**Checked:** `cargo xtask ci` (fmt, clippy, test, assets, layers, wasm) passes locally on the final commit, rebased on 7fef8cb: 2,219 tests, 0 failed. Also driven in a freshly built app over the control channel: eight items along an L-shaped polyline turn the corner; `ARRAYEDIT` to 4 items with alignment off respaces them and stops the turning; `ARRAYPATH` typed at the command line around a circle gives one associative array of six radial ticks ("Type = Path  Associative = Yes").

